select $t$Hamilton Prep$t$, $t$plr-ivoryos==1.0.0b2$t$, $t$Prep$t$, $t$plr_ivoryos.hamilton$t$, (select contributor_id from modules where id = 422),
  array['Windows','Linux','macOS']::text[], (select id from devices where vendor = 'Hamilton' and name = 'Prep'),
  array['']::text[], false, false, $j$[{"name": "simulated", "type": "bool", "default": true}, {"name": "host", "type": "str"}, {"name": "port", "type": "int", "default": 2000}, {"name": "worktable", "type": "str", "default": "worktable.json"}, {"name": "step_delay_s", "type": "float", "default": 0}]$j$::jsonb, false, '{}'::text[], $t$A Hamilton Prep through PyLabRobot 1.0. The robot is one instrument and each thing fitted to it another (prep_pipettes, prep_head8, prep_core_grippers, prep_heater_shaker, prep_lights), each with PyLabRobot's own steps; labware goes in its eight spots, 0 to 7. Simulated (the default) is PyLabRobot's own Prep simulator; for a real Prep, untick simulated and give host. Beta: pinned to PyLabRobot 1.0.0b1, run in simulation only. Decks on one computer share one Python environment, so this replaces the PyLabRobot that any older plr-ivoryos driver there needs. Add the Labware plugin after this driver to see and edit the worktable.$t$, (select s->$t$Prep$t$ from _schemas),
